<item>
            <title>[LU-20689] utils: support decimal (SI) size units</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-20689</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;1) Support decimal (SI) size suffixes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every KMGTPE suffix is a power of two today, so &quot;1G&quot;, &quot;1GiB&quot; and &quot;1GB&quot; all mean 1073741824. &#160;There is no way to enter a size in the decimal units that capacity documents are written in, and a value copied from&lt;br/&gt;
one is over-provisioned by 7.4% at GB and 10% at TB. &#160;A trailing &quot;B&quot; should select decimal units:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&#160; &#160; 1G, 1GiB &#160;-&amp;gt; &#160;2^30 &#160; (unchanged)&lt;br/&gt;
&#160; &#160; 1GB &#160; &#160; &#160; -&amp;gt; &#160;10^9&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the meaning the kernel side already has: sysfs_memparse() -&amp;gt; string_to_blksize() in lustre/obdclass/lprocfs_status.c reads &quot;MB&quot; as 10^6, so &quot;lctl set_param llite.*.pcc_async_threshold=5MB&quot; is 5000000&lt;br/&gt;
while &quot;lfs setquota -B 5MB&quot; is 5242880 today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The suffix text alone decides, so no new flag or API is needed and every caller of llapi_parse_size() behaves the same way: lfs setstripe (and migrate, mirror create/extend/split/delete, which share its option parser), lfs find, lfs getstripe, lfs ladvise, lfs mirror resync, lfs setquota, llsom_sync and lhsmtool_posix - 19 call sites, 43 command/option combinations in total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;2) Reject anything that is not a standard spelling.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Only the first character after the suffix is checked, so &quot;1GBjunk&quot; and &quot;1Gi&quot; are silently accepted as 1 GiB. &#160;Accept exactly &quot;1G&quot;, &quot;1GB&quot; and &quot;1GiB&quot;, in either case, and reject the rest. &#160;The same pass also refuses a value that would overflow 64 bits once scaled by its unit, which the current per-suffix bit-mask checks cannot express for a&lt;br/&gt;
10^3-based unit.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
